Vendored skills — attribution
==============================

Several core process skills under this directory are vendored (a curated,
lightly trimmed copy) from:

  superpowers — https://github.com/obra/superpowers
  Copyright (c) obra (Jesse Vincent) and contributors
  License: MIT

The obra/superpowers skills are redistributed under the terms of the MIT
License. The MIT permission notice is reproduced below. Only operational content
was kept; authoring/meta/test files and non–Claude-Code tool references were
removed. Skill methodology is unchanged.

Vendored skills from obra/superpowers:
  brainstorming, executing-plans, test-driven-development,
  systematic-debugging, verification-before-completion,
  dispatching-parallel-agents, subagent-driven-development,
  using-git-worktrees, using-superpowers

Note: finishing-a-development-branch was vendored previously but has been
dropped — hero-mmt-kit intentionally does not automate merge/PR/branch-cleanup
decisions; those stay with the human.

Note on hero-planning: this hero-mmt-kit-authored skill incorporates adapted
plan-structuring methodology from obra/superpowers' former writing-plans skill,
including change-surface mapping, task decomposition, actionable task structure,
and placeholder avoidance. That adapted methodology remains under MIT. The
requirements ledger, requirement-to-task coverage model, implementation-only
boundary, and human-controlled cross-session handoff are hero-mmt-kit design
choices.

Note on hero-reviewing: this hero-mmt-kit-authored skill incorporates adapted
methodology from obra/superpowers' former requesting-code-review and
receiving-code-review skills, including fresh reviewer context, evidence-first
review, technical evaluation before accepting feedback, severity calibration,
compatibility checks, scope/YAGNI checks, and evidence-based pushback. That
adapted methodology remains under MIT. The read-only stage boundary, explicit
local/commit/ref target model, four-way feedback classification, parent
validation of delegated findings, and user-approved cross-session correction
handoff are hero-mmt-kit design choices.

Note on subagent-driven-development: this skill received a selective backport of
newer upstream additions (durable progress ledger, task-brief/review-package
scripts, explicit model-selection discipline, reviewer anti-pre-judging guidance)
on top of hero-mmt-kit's own Review Budget tiering system, which upstream does not
have. The backported additions remain obra/superpowers-origin content under MIT;
Review Budget tiering is a hero-mmt-kit original design choice.

Note on systematic-debugging: this skill received a hero-mmt-kit-original
supporting technique, `instrumented-debugging.md` (structured console/log
checkpoint placement for a reproducible API/logic bug, used in place of
attaching a debugger), added alongside the vendored `root-cause-tracing.md`,
`defense-in-depth.md`, and `condition-based-waiting.md` technique files. This
addition is a hero-mmt-kit design choice; the vendored Phase 1-4 process and
the other supporting files remain unchanged obra/superpowers-origin content
under MIT.

Framework-authored skills shipped by hero-mmt-kit:
  using-hero, hero-planning, hero-coding, hero-reviewing,
  hero-unit-test, hero-security, hero-mr-review, hero-strict, hero-report

Design / visual-direction skills are NOT vendored — they are referenced in
skills.manifest.json and installed from their original sources on demand.

Concise reporting principles in the hero-* workflow skills are inspired by the
compression-mode concept from:

  caveman — https://github.com/JuliusBrussee/caveman
  Copyright (c) Julius Brussee
  License: MIT

hero-mmt-kit no longer ships concise-output as a standalone skill. Its idea
(compress output, preserve technical accuracy, revert to full clarity for
safety-critical content) is integrated directly into using-hero and the hero-*
reporting guidance. No text was copied verbatim; only the underlying concept
was adapted. Reproduced below per MIT attribution practice.

